    "title": "REPORT: Kawhi Leonard Once Sought Extra $15M In Endorsements During Free Agency Talks With Raptors, Fueling Ongoing Allegations - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Shabiha Akter ,\n27 minutes ago\nKawhi Leonard (Image Credits: Imagn) Los Angeles Clippers star Kawhi Leonard recently traveled to China to promote basketball. There, he immersed himself in local culture by practicing Wing Chun martial arts and connecting with communities. However, his overseas trip was quickly overshadowed by a bombshell report on his off-court dealings.\nJournalist Pablo Torre shared thousands of pages of legal documents in his show that published Wednesday. According to his claim, Leonard may have been paid as much as $28 million for a “no-show job” with a company tied to Clippers owner Steve Ballmer.\nNBA insider John Gambardo further claimed that Leonard once asked Toronto Raptors owner Larry Tanenbaum to secure an additional $15 million in endorsements during his free agency process.\nReports suggest that both Leonard and the Clippers could face serious consequences if the allegations are proven true. NBA spokesperson Mike Bass addressed the situation, confirming that an investigation is underway.\n“We are aware of this morning’s media report regarding the LA Clippers and are commencing an investigation ,” Bass said.\nClippers Stand Firm In Defense Of Kawhi Leonard Amid Endorsement Allegations Kawhi Leonard (Image Credits: Imagn ) Both Ballmer and the Clippers organization denied any wrongdoing or unusual practices in connection with Leonard’s endorsement deals.\n“ There is nothing unusual or untoward about team sponsors doing endorsement deals with players on the same team. Neither Steve nor the Clippers organization had any oversight of Kawhi’s independent endorsement agreement with Aspiration. To say otherwise is flat-out wrong ,” the Clippers said in a statement.\nThe allegations cast uncertainty over Leonard’s reputation and the Clippers’ standing. The off-court controversy could shape both his future and the team’s trajectory in the coming season.\nThe post originally published on .",
